\title{The Two-Parameter $\pm$-Frame: Coefficients of Binary Cyclotomic
Polynomials as Lattice Points in a Balance Box}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{20 August 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
We develop, from first principles, a self-contained theory of the coefficients of the
\emph{$\pm$-frame} $\Phi_n \in \ZZ[X]$ --- the $n$-th cyclotomic polynomial regarded as a
signed coefficient frame --- in the one- and two-parameter cases, and isolate the precise
mechanism bounding those coefficients from below. For $n=p$ prime, every coefficient of
$\Phi_p$ is $0$ or $1$. For $n = pq$ with $p \neq q$ prime we prove the closed formula
\[
  \Phi_{pq}(X)\,(X^{pq}-1) \;=\; (X-1)\,G_{p,q}(X),
  \qquad
  G_{p,q}(X) = \Bigl(\sum_{i<q}X^{ip}\Bigr)\Bigl(\sum_{j<p}X^{jq}\Bigr),
\]
which converts the coefficient problem into a question about integer points of a
two-dimensional region: the coefficient of $X^n$ in $G_{p,q}$ counts the lattice points
$(i,j)$ of the \emph{balance box} $[0,q)\times[0,p)$ on the line $ip+jq=n$. The arithmetic
core is a uniqueness statement --- a coprime line meets the balance box at most once. From
it we deduce Migotti's theorem ($\Phi_{pq}$ has all coefficients in $\{-1,0,1\}$), the exact
sign pattern as the discrete derivative of the indicator of the numerical semigroup
$\langle p,q\rangle$, the balance law $\sum_k [X^k]\Phi_{pq}=1$, sharpness of the bound $-1$
for \emph{every} semiprime, Sylvester's symmetry and gap count $\tfrac12(p-1)(q-1)$, and
palindromicity. We exhibit the exact boundary of the method: for the non-coprime steps
$2,4$ the line $2i+4j=4$ meets the box $[0,4)\times[0,2)$ twice. Finally we explain why three
parameters are expected to destroy the bound: the ternary analogue replaces ``a line inside a
rectangle'' by ``a plane inside a three-dimensional box'', where multiplicity one is no longer
forced.
\end{abstract}

